29. Scheduled Incremental Remediation Mode
Objective

This task runs on a recurring schedule. Each execution must remediate a maximum of ONE dependency.

Do not attempt to complete all dependency upgrades in a single scheduled execution.

The objective is to make measurable, independently reviewable progress across multiple scheduled runs while maintaining all existing security, testing, ACU and audit constraints.

29.1 One Dependency Per Scheduled Run

For each scheduled execution:

Read docs/compliance/remediation-progress.md, if it exists.
Inspect the current repository branch and existing remediation PRs.
Run the required baseline checks and vulnerability scan when permitted by the conditions below.
Check whether the CVE target has already been achieved.
Select exactly one eligible dependency.
Attempt its upgrade within the existing limits.
Open at most one remediation or escalation PR during this run.
Stop execution immediately after completing or aborting that dependency.

Never start a second dependency upgrade during the same scheduled execution.

29.2 Persistent Progress Tracking

Maintain the following file:

docs/compliance/remediation-progress.md

Record:

Initial CVE count.
Latest verified CVE count.
Last scan date.
Completed dependencies and their versions.
Skipped dependencies and their reasons.
Open remediation PRs.
Merged remediation PRs.
Dependencies awaiting manual review.
Remaining eligible upgrade candidates.
Overall remediation status.

Update this file in the same PR as the corresponding dependency upgrade or escalation audit.

Do not mark an upgrade as merged until the repository confirms that the PR has actually been merged.

Use the repository's actual Git and PR state as the source of truth. Do not rely exclusively on the progress file.

29.3 Previous PR Check

Before starting a new dependency upgrade, inspect outstanding remediation PRs.

If a previous remediation PR is still open, draft, or awaiting review:

Do not start another dependency upgrade.
Do not create a duplicate PR.
Record that the scheduled execution is waiting for the previous PR.
Stop the current execution.

If the previous PR was merged:

Start from the latest merged repository state.
Recalculate the current CVE count using the existing scanner.
Continue only if the target has not been achieved.

If a previous PR was closed without merging:

Inspect its recorded outcome.
Do not automatically repeat the same failed upgrade.
Follow the recorded escalation or retry decision.
Retry only when there is a specific, justified change in circumstances.
29.4 Single-Dependency Transaction

Treat each dependency upgrade as an independent transaction.

The transaction consists of:

Selecting the dependency.
Recording its current and target versions.
Applying the upgrade.
Making permitted compatibility changes.
Updating required integration tests.
Running the required validation.
Scanning for vulnerabilities.
Updating the progress file.
Opening one focused PR.

Do not begin the next transaction during the same scheduled run.

If validation fails within the permitted limits, revert the attempted implementation and follow the existing escalation procedure.

29.5 PR and Merge Gate

After a successful upgrade:

Open one focused PR containing the dependency upgrade, necessary code changes, tests and progress update.
Stop the current scheduled execution.
Wait for the PR to be merged before attempting another dependency.

After an aborted upgrade:

Open one audit PR containing the dated escalation document and the customized GitHub Copilot prompt.
Update the progress file.
Stop the current execution.

Do not assume that opening a PR means the remediation has been applied to the main branch.

29.6 Scheduled Execution Stop Conditions

At the beginning of every scheduled run, check these conditions in order:

If the latest verified CVE count is below five and the result is confirmed against the current repository state, stop.
If an earlier remediation PR remains unmerged, stop and wait.
If no eligible dependency upgrades remain, stop and report the remaining vulnerabilities.
Otherwise, process exactly one dependency.

After completing an upgrade, stop even if other eligible dependencies remain.

Do not bypass these conditions to meet an arbitrary number of upgrades per week.

29.7 Idempotency

Repeated scheduled executions must not:

Create duplicate remediation PRs.
Reapply an already merged dependency upgrade.
Reattempt an aborted upgrade without a documented reason.
Overwrite existing escalation documents.
Lose the historical remediation record.
Upgrade additional dependencies after the CVE target is achieved.

If the progress file and actual repository state disagree, reconcile them using verified Git and PR information before proceeding.

29.8 Per-Run Final Report

At the end of each scheduled execution, report:

Scheduled execution date.
Starting CVE count.
Dependency selected.
Dependency upgrade outcome.
Tests executed and their results.
Ending CVE count, if a new scan was completed.
PR URL, if created.
Whether the run stopped because of the CVE target, an open PR, an aborted upgrade or candidate exhaustion.
The next action required.

Never claim that a vulnerability has been remediated on the main branch while its upgrade PR is still unmerged.
